      "name": "Week 4 - Reinforcement Learning-Based Amharic Tokenization",
      "description": "\nIntroduce residents to the practical implementation, fine-tuning, evaluation, and\noptimization of Large Language Models.\n\u25cf Residents will understand the capabilities, limitations, risks, and operational\nrequirements of LLM-based systems.\n\u25cf The project will focus on building an LLM-based Leyu task reviewer that evaluates\nsubmitted tasks and recommends whether each task should be accepted or rejected.\n\u25cf Residents will develop either a functional prototype or a fine-tuned model tailored to the\nLeyu review use case.\n\u25cf The final system should provide a decision, confidence score, and clear explanation for\neach reviewed task.\n\n\nThis project introduces residents to the practical implementation, fine-tuning, evaluation, and optimization of Large Language Models (LLMs). Residents will explore the capabilities, limitations, risks, and operational requirements of LLM-based systems by developing a Leyu task-review solution that evaluates submitted tasks and recommends whether each submission should be accepted or rejected. They will build either a functional LLM-based prototype or a fine-tuned model tailored to the Leyu review use case. The final system should provide an acceptance or rejection decision, a confidence score, and a clear explanation for each reviewed task.\n\n\n\u25cf Introduce residents to tokenization challenges in Amharic and the use of reinforcement\nlearning to optimize token boundaries and vocabulary selection.\n\u25cf Residents will develop an experimental tokenizer that represents Amharic text more\nefficiently than general-purpose multilingual tokenizers.\n\u25cf The project will focus on reducing token fragmentation while preserving meaningful\nAmharic words, roots, affixes, and morphemes.\n\u25cf Residents will compare the reinforcement learning-based tokenizer with BPE,\nWordPiece, and SentencePiece baselines\n\nThis project introduces residents to tokenization challenges in Amharic and the use of reinforcement learning to optimize token boundaries and vocabulary selection. Residents will develop an experimental tokenizer designed to represent Amharic text more efficiently than general-purpose multilingual tokenizers, with a focus on reducing token fragmentation while preserving meaningful words, roots, affixes, and morphemes. They will evaluate the proposed reinforcement learning-based tokenizer and compare its performance with established approaches such as Byte Pair Encoding (BPE), WordPiece, and SentencePiece.\n\n\nnot prodcject it is modeul\n\nThis module introduces residents to tokenization challenges in Amharic and the use of reinforcement learning to optimize token boundaries and vocabulary selection. Residents will develop an experimental tokenizer that represents Amharic text more efficiently than general-purpose multilingual tokenizers, focusing on reducing token fragmentation while preserving meaningful words, roots, affixes, and morphemes. They will evaluate the reinforcement learning-based tokenizer and compare its performance with established approaches such as Byte Pair Encoding (BPE), WordPiece, and SentencePiece.",
